WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.962
A vector field assigns a vector to every point in a region

00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:13.706
In this radial field, the output repeats the position coordinates, so arrows point away from the origin and become longer farther from it

00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:16.367
Position and assigned vector are different roles.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:22.636
Evaluate a field by substituting the location into both component functions

00:00:22.636 --> 00:00:29.412
At two, one, the vector is two, one; at negative one, two, it changes accordingly

00:00:29.412 --> 00:00:34.833
These outputs describe local direction and magnitude rather than another scalar height.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:40.879
Swapping the coordinates and negating the first creates a rotational field

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:43.899
At two, zero, its arrow points upward

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:51.233
Around a circle, the field is tangent to the path, contrasting with the radial field's perpendicular arrows.

00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:59.421
A constant field assigns the same vector everywhere, so every arrow points right with the same length

00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:08.333
Fields may model force or velocity, but the interpretation and units must come from the model; arrows alone do not specify them.
